  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/pseudoxiphophorus_anzuetoi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Pseudoxiphophorus anzuetoi stays much smaller than P. bimaculatus and becomes only 6-7 cm long. It differs from the much better known congeneric by the attractive blue spots on the head.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/pseudoxiphophorus_anzuetoi_en/",
      "html": "<p>A very unusual livebearer, possibly the first specimens ever traded worldwide. The specimens offered are German bred; their ancestors were brought in by a private importation. Available size: 5-6 cm. Available in limited numbers only.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/pseudoxiphophorus_anzuetoi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Pseudoxiphophorus anzuetoi originates from Guatemala, El Salvador, and Honduras.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/pseudoxiphophorus_anzuetoi_en/",
      "html": "<p>It inhabits rocky rivers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/pseudoxiphophorus_anzuetoi_en/",
      "html": "<p>This species, like all other species of Pseudoxiphophorus, was formerly placed in the genus Heterandria for anatomical reasons. As a result, one of the largest livebearers, Pseudoxiphophorus bimaculatus, in which females can attain a maximum length of more than 15 cm, and one of the smallest, Heterandria formosa, were placed in the same genus. Nowadays most scientists follow the opinion that Pseudoxiphophorus is a genus of its own.</p>"
    }
